1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,592
Hello!

2
00:00:00,592 --> 00:00:04,414
Hangeul is one of the most scientific writing systems in the world.

3
00:00:04,414 --> 00:00:04,756
Today,

4
00:00:04,756 --> 00:00:09,320
let's discover how it was born and unlock the secret of its eight simple vowels.

5
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,391
Long ago, Koreans had no script of their own,

6
00:00:12,391 --> 00:00:14,708
so they borrowed complex Chinese characters.

7
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:18,080
Hanja was so hard that most common people could not read at all.

8
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:21,979
Seeing his people suffer because they could not read,

9
00:00:21,979 --> 00:00:24,937
King Sejong was deeply moved. 'Out of compassion,

10
00:00:24,937 --> 00:00:27,110
I create twenty-eight new letters. '

11
00:00:27,810 --> 00:00:29,069
Finally, in 1443,

12
00:00:29,069 --> 00:00:33,068
Hunminjeongeum was born — a script anyone could learn.

13
00:00:33,068 --> 00:00:36,105
The wise learned it in a morning; anyone,

14
00:00:36,105 --> 00:00:37,290
within ten days.

15
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:42,274
Hangeul's vowels are modeled on three elements of the universe: heaven,

16
00:00:42,274 --> 00:00:44,688
earth, and human. Heaven is a round dot,

17
00:00:44,688 --> 00:00:46,438
earth a flat horizontal line,

18
00:00:46,438 --> 00:00:48,550
and human an upright vertical line.

19
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:54,234
Add the heaven dot to the right of the human line and you get the bright sound 'ㅏ',

20
00:00:54,234 --> 00:00:57,057
the rising sun in the east. Put it on the left,

21
00:00:57,057 --> 00:00:58,919
and you get the dark sound 'ㅓ',

22
00:00:58,919 --> 00:01:00,600
the setting sun in the west.

23
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:05,889
Place the dot above the earth line and you get 'ㅗ'; below it,

24
00:01:05,889 --> 00:01:06,190
'ㅜ'.

25
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:09,650
Rising above is bright; sinking below is dark.

26
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:12,338
So bright vowels pair with bright,

27
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:13,859
and dark vowels with dark.

28
00:01:13,859 --> 00:01:16,490
This beautiful order is called vowel harmony.

29
00:01:17,190 --> 00:01:20,137
Now let's make some sounds.

30
00:01:20,137 --> 00:01:25,267
Korean has eight simple vowels. Unlike English,

31
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:31,271
the secret is to keep your mouth shape perfectly still.

32
00:01:31,271 --> 00:01:36,510
Let's say them together. ㅏ, ㅓ, ㅗ, ㅜ, ㅡ, ㅣ, ㅐ, ㅔ.

33
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:39,818
First, 'ㅏ' and 'ㅓ'. For both,

34
00:01:39,818 --> 00:01:44,403
drop your jaw to open wide and keep your lips flat.

35
00:01:44,403 --> 00:01:48,270
Open wider for 'ㅏ', a bit narrower for 'ㅓ'.

36
00:01:48,970 --> 00:01:50,279
For 'ㅗ' and 'ㅜ',

37
00:01:50,279 --> 00:01:55,104
round your lips like holding a straw and push them forward.

38
00:01:55,104 --> 00:01:59,030
'ㅗ' is a little bigger, 'ㅜ' smaller and tighter.

39
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:00,323
For 'ㅡ',

40
00:02:00,323 --> 00:02:04,175
stretch your lips flat sideways like a forced smile.

41
00:02:04,175 --> 00:02:04,768
For 'ㅣ',

42
00:02:04,768 --> 00:02:08,250
pull them even wider so your teeth almost show.

43
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:10,396
Finally, 'ㅐ' and 'ㅔ'.

44
00:02:10,396 --> 00:02:13,909
Open your mouth moderately with the tongue forward.

45
00:02:13,909 --> 00:02:15,080
In modern Korean,

46
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,490
the two sounds are almost the same.

47
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:20,060
Check in a mirror!

48
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:27,020
Your lips should round only for 'ㅗ' and 'ㅜ' — never for 'ㅓ' or 'ㅡ'.

49
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:30,958
Practice today's vowels in front of a mirror every day.

50
00:02:30,958 --> 00:02:33,960
Subscribe and like for more fun Hangeul adventures.

51
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:35,020
See you next time!

